S:https://amgueddfa.cymru/casgliadau/caneuongwerin/?id=12 (collected by D. Roy Saer)
N:Sung by E. Thomas Evans (born 1883), a farmer, of Brynhyfryd, Foel Garthbeibio, Montgomeryshire; recorded 27.6.63 (St Fagans tape 526). He learnt it when he was about 12 from an old farm servant in Garthbeibio. A drunkard's soliloquy: the first verse has long been popular all over Wales, but the second had not been printed before. The link with drink also shows in the version Robert Roberts, Tai'r Felin, used to sing ("Cân Sobri"), and there is more on the song in the Journal of the Welsh Folk Song Society, vols. 1 (as "Yr Eira"), 2 and 4.
N:In verse 1 the repeated part is sung once, so each of its lines comes twice. In verse 2 every line is sung twice, the repeated part going round twice (the score marks it "D.S. (x2)"). The bracketed E in bars 5 and 7 is in brackets on the score. In bars 6, 8, 9 and 11 the score also gives three plain quavers in place of the crotchet and quaver at the start of the bar (G G E, E E E, A A B), for verses with an extra syllable; bar 3 has that form.
